  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 PM CST Tue Nov 17 2026\n\nCurrent conditions/Tonight:\n\nUpper level ridging continues to hold over the region high pressure \nlingering over the Ohio River Valley. Farther west over the \nnorthern Rockies a surface low has taken shape but will not \nsee much movement until an upper level trough kicks it into \ngear tomorrow. This leaves us with southwesterly flow staying \nsteady allowing for warm air advection and weak isentropic lift \nover the Northland. Satellite shows that this weak lift has \nmanifested stratus that has engulfed most of the Northland with \nsome erosion found over NW WI. Dry air above the moist layer \ncoupled with some weak forcing and lowering of the cloud deck \ncould result in sporadic areas of drizzle. Overall, expect our \nquiet weather and above normal temperatures to continue. \nOvernight low temps were given a slight bump as well given the \nwarm air advection and widespread cloud cover expected. \n\nWednesday/Thursday:\n\nNot a lot of change as we head into tomorrow. An upper level trough \nmoves out of the Pacific Northwest and will help to propagate the \nlow pressure over the Northern Rockies into the Northern Plains. In \nresponse we will see a tightening pressure gradient over the region \nwith slightly strong winds out of the southwest. Continued cloud \ncover is expected with some areas of drizzle possible across \nnorthern MN. On Thursday the aforementioned surface low moves \nthrough NW MN and into Ontario. This track does not provide much \nsupport for rain across the Borderlands with probabilities dropping \nto 30-40%. \n\nFriday-Weekend:\n\nA much deeper trough is set to dig through the Front Range on Friday \nejecting another low pressure into the the Upper Midwest. Model \nguidance is settling in on a similar storm track as Thursday's \nsystem. However, since the starting point is a bit further \nsouth we should see some increased precipitation chances despite\nthe low largely moving north of the region. The deformation \nzone will elude the region but the trailing cold front will act \nas ample forcing for rain to sweep across the region. A \nsecondary low could also form within the frontal boundary as we \nhead into Saturday, or at the very least some impressive FGEN \nbanding to promote some better precipitation rates. Initial \nonset precipitation from the parent low pressure will begin \nFriday with the possible banded precipitation taking shape on \nSaturday. Fairly high confidence in the set up for Friday \nleading to precipitation amounts largely less than 0.25\" across \nnorthern MN. Placement of the banded precipitation has lower \nconfidence but could provide an additional 0.50-0.75 of rain \nwith NW WI currently having the best odds of seeing this \noutcome. \n\nSunday the system largely departs to the east with wrap around \nmoisture still swirling over the Northland. By this time we will see \nmuch colder air filtering in from the northwest that would support \nthe production of snow. Questions still remain on how much lingering \nmoisture the atmosphere will have to play with but the current \nforecast suggests accumulations of half an inch will be possible \nacross the Borderlands with some accumulation also possible along \nthe South Shore as lake effect process begins. \n\nNext Week:\n\nA much cooler air mass settles in to start the next work week with \nhighs hovering in the low 30s and the overnight lows in the teens.",
